    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L09-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Graphs of Cosine and Sine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For the equation given, predict what the graph looks like, and then check your prediction using technology: \\(y=\\theta+\\cos(\\theta)\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L09-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Graphs of Cosine and Sine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Using the unit circle, we can make sense of \\(\\cos(\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(\\theta)\\) for any angle measure \\(\\theta\\) between 0 and \\(2\\pi\\) radians. For an angle \\(\\theta\\) starting at the positive \\(x\\)-axis, there is a point \\(C\\) where…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L09-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Graphs of Cosine and Sine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "But what if we wanted to think about just the horizontal position of point \\(C\\) as \\(\\theta\\) goes from 0 to \\(2\\pi\\)? The horizontal location is defined by the \\(x\\)-coordinate, which is \\(\\cos(\\theta)\\). If we graph \\(y=\\cos(\\theta)\\),…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L09-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Graphs of Cosine and Sine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This graph is 1 when \\(\\theta\\) is 0 because the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the point at 0 radians on the unit circle is \\((1,0)\\). The graph then decreases to -1 (the smallest \\(x\\)-value on the unit circle) before increasing back to 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L09-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Graphs of Cosine and Sine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can do the same for the \\(y\\)-coordinate of a point on the unit circle by graphing \\(y=\\sin(\\theta)\\):",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L09-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Graphs of Cosine and Sine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This graph is 0 when \\(\\theta\\) is 0, increases to 1 (the greatest \\(y\\)-value on the unit circle), then decreases to -1 before returning to 0.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L09-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Graphs of Cosine and Sine",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function whose values repeat at regular intervals. If \\(f\\) is a periodic function then there is a number \\(p\\), called the period, so that \\(f(x + p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
